<header><p class="st-kicker">other · Italy</p><h1 class="st-title">Lordship of Faenza</h1><p class="st-meta">1313 CE – 1501 CE (approx.) · capital Faenza</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The Manfredi lordship of Faenza in Romagna, the town whose glazed pottery gave Europe the word 'faience'.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The Manfredi family led Faenza's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-welf">Guelphs</a> in the 13th century, and in 1313 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francesco-manfredi">Francesco Manfredi</a> became lord of the city. The family ruled as papal vicars, though they lost and regained the city several times in the 14th century.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The Manfredi governed Faenza and neighbouring towns such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imola">Imola</a> for a time, often serving as condottieri for <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/duchy-of-florence">Florence</a>, <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/duchy-of-milan">Milan</a> or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a>. The town's maiolica workshops flourished in the 15th century, and their glazed earthenware gave French and English the name faience. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carlo-ii-manfredi">Carlo II Manfredi</a> rebuilt the cathedral and the town centre in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renaissance">Renaissance</a> style.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 1501 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cesare-borgia">Cesare Borgia</a> besieged Faenza, which surrendered; the young lord <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/astorre-iii-manfredi">Astorre III Manfredi</a> was taken to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> and murdered in 1502. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> held Faenza until 1509, when it passed to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/papal-states">Papal States</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="other, 1313 CE – 1501 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M26 24 H74 V52 Q74 74 50 84 Q26 74 26 52 Z"/><path d="M50 24 V84 M26 46 H74"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">other</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Italy</p><div class=